  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA few isolated showers remain along the windshift/leading edge\nof drier air, which is currently working into the ridges. The\nmain shortwave trough axis rotates through this morning, which\nwill be followed by 500mb ridging through Tuesday. Isolated\npatches of fog are possible this morning due to the moist \nground, but decreasing dewpoints and lingering light mixing may \ntend to limit this somewhat.\n\nCrossing surface high pressure will then provide dry weather\ntoday into at least Tuesday evening. Lingering lower heights\naloft will support high temperatures some 5 to 8 degrees below\nnormal today. With the surface high overhead tonight, a clear\nand seasonably cool night is forecast, with lows in the 40s \nexpected in the ridges and north of US-422. Temperatures climb \nback above normal Tuesday in 850mb warm advection behind the \ndeparting high.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe next chance of precipitation now appears a bit more likely\nto hold off until late Tuesday night or Wednesday morning, as\nthe next shortwave trough skirts the northern Great Lakes. Ensemble\nclusters show some agreement in keeping elevated, quasi-zonal \n500mb heights over the Ohio Valley. This may help to hang an \nassociated surface front closer to the shores of the eastern \nGreat Lakes into Thursday before it potentially gets a southward\npush. Machine-learning guidance continues to suggest lower-end\nsevere and flooding threats.\n\nThe high heights should also keep temperatures above normal, \nand with low-level southwest flow pushing dewpoints well into \nthe 60s to around 70, an increase in heat stress is possible. \nProbabilistic HeatRisk guidance shows 50-90% chances for \nModerate (level 2 of 4) or higher near and south of Pittsburgh \nWednesday, and CWA- wide on Thursday. Temperatures may then cool\nslightly Friday behind the front.",
